@ 17.7.2.1.5 - Early Modern English to American English
# citation: Pogostick Man, from FireSpeakerWiki contributors (2013), “English sound changes”. FireSpeakerWiki. Stuff regarding syllabification (e.g., of /ɹ/) and hiatus Loss of pretonic /ə/ in #U (ongoing)
	æ > æ:[+long] (e.g., NYC) or ɑ:[+long] (e.g.,:[+back]oston) / :{ _F:[-voice](“words which change vary between dialect͡s”), _N:[-voice](“words which change vary between dialect͡s”) }:
	ɒ > ɒ:[+long] / _F:[-voice]
	ɒ:[+long] > ɔ:[+long] / _F:[-voice]
	{ æ:[+long], ɑ:[+long], ɔ:[+long] } > { æə~eə, ɑ, ɔ }
	ʍ > w (regional)
	l > ɫ / in some conditions
	ɪ > i / _# when unstressed
	{t,d} > ɾ / V_V:[-stress]
	{ i, u, e } > { ɪ, ʊ, ɛ } / _ɹ
	{ o, ɔ } > { ɔ, ɒ } / _ɹ (most dialect͡s have at least one if not both)
	æ > ɛ / _ɹ
	j > ∅ / :{ θ_ when in onset position, s_ when in onset position, z_ when in onset position, l_ when in onset position, n_ when in onset position, t_ when in onset position, d_ when in onset position }:
	ɒ > ɔ / _[+ cons, - fr, + bk, + hi, - lo] (partial)
	æ > ɛə / :{ _n“and others depending on dialect”, _m“and others depending on dialect” }:
	eə > e:[+long]
	iə > ɪ (ongoing)
	w > ∅ / C_ɹ for some C (toward(s), quart(er), sword)
	t > ∅ / f_n̩